# Federated Learning Framework for Smart Healthcare Diagnostics

A privacy-preserving, personalized Federated Learning (FL) framework that lets hospitals with different medical specialties collaboratively train a diagnostic model **without ever sharing raw patient data**.

> Semester project | Apex Institute of Technology | [Your University Name] | [Month, Year]

---

## Overview

Hospitals hold valuable patient data, but privacy regulations prevent them from pooling it. Smaller or specialty-focused hospitals also rarely have enough diverse data to train accurate diagnostic models alone.

This project shows how multiple hospitals can train a shared model while keeping all patient data local, and then personalize that model for their own patient population. Differential Privacy protects the model updates that are shared.

### Key Idea
1. Each hospital trains locally; raw data never leaves the hospital.
2. Only model weights are sent to a central server, which combines them using **Federated Averaging (FedAvg)**.
3. Each hospital **personalizes** the shared global model to its own specialty.
4. **Differential Privacy** (gradient clipping + Gaussian noise) is applied to shared updates.

### Novelty
Most existing work treats hospital differences as abstract statistical (non-IID) splits. This project grounds heterogeneity in **real medical specialty differences** (Respiratory, Cardiology, Infectious Disease, General) and evaluates how a naive global model, personalization, and Differential Privacy behave under those conditions.

---

## Key Findings

| Hospital | Global-only Accuracy | Personalized Accuracy |
|---|---|---|
| Respiratory | ~0% | ~100% |
| Cardiology | ~0% | ~100% |
| Infectious Disease | ~9% | ~90% |
| General | ~97% | ~100% |

- The shared global model is dominated by the hospital with the most data (General) and **fails almost completely** on smaller specialty hospitals.
- A lightweight personalization step resolves this almost entirely.
- Differential Privacy adds a measurable accuracy cost, most visible on the General hospital (about 74–79% with DP enabled).

*Exact numbers may vary slightly between runs and environments.*

